 Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "SD", "instrModeDescrshort": "DL-SD", "instrModeDescr": "Distance Learning-Synchronous", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "COGST", "catalogNbr": "1101", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "CS", "catalogNbr": "1710", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "HD", "catalogNbr": "1102", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "LING", "catalogNbr": "1170", "type": "C"}, {"subject": "PSYCH", "catalogNbr": "1102", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "6WK", "sessionBeginDt": "06/23/2025", "sessionEndDt": "08/05/2025", "sessionLong": "Six Week Summer", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "This course provides an introduction to the science of the mind. Everyone knows what it's like to think and perceive, but this subjective experience provides little insight into how minds emerge from physical entities like brains. To address this issue, cognitive science integrates work from at least five disciplines: Psychology, Neuroscience, Computer Science, Linguistics, and Philosophy. This course introduces students to the insights these disciplines offer into the workings of the mind by exploring visual perception, attention, memory, learning, problem solving, language, and consciousness.\u00a0", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ETM-AS) (KCM-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": null, "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall, Summer.", "catalogComments": "A 1 credit Writing in the Majors sections is available,\u00a0PHIL 1621.", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": null, "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": "2025FA, 2025SU, 2024FA, 2024SU", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}, {"strm": 2902, "crseId": 364457, "crseOfferNbr": 1, "subject": "PHIL", "catalogNbr": "2621", "titleShort": "Minds and Machines", "titleLong": "Minds and Machines", "crseAttrs": [], "crseAttrValueGroups": [], "enrollGroups": [{"classSections": [{"ssrComponent": "LEC", "ssrComponentLong": "Lecture", "section": "001", "classNbr": 1422, "meetings": [{"classMtgNbr": 1, "timeStart": "02:30PM", "timeEnd": "03:45PM", "startDt": "06/23/2025", "endDt": "08/01/2025", "instructors": [{"instrAssignSeq": 5, "netid": "eag264", "firstName": "Eve", "middleName": "Antoinette", "lastName": "Dietl"}], "pattern": "MTWRF", "meetingTopicDescription": ""}], "notes": [{"classNotesSeq": 1, "descrlong": "This Summer Session class is offered by the School of Continuing Education and Summer Sessions. For details visit: https://sce.cornell.edu/courses/roster."}], "campus": "MAIN", "campusDescr": "Ithaca", "location": "ITH", "locationDescr": "Ithaca, NY (Main Campus)", "startDt": "06/23/2025", "endDt": "08/05/2025", "addConsent": "N", "addConsentDescr": "No Special Consent Required", "isComponentGraded": true, "crseAttrs": [], "instructionMode": "SD", "instrModeDescrshort": "DL-SD", "instrModeDescr": "Distance Learning-Synchronous", "topicDescription": "", "openStatus": "O", "exploreCriteriaIds": [], "materials": []}], "unitsMinimum": 3, "unitsMaximum": 3, "componentsOptional": [], "componentsRequired": ["LEC"], "gradingBasis": "OPT", "gradingBasisShort": "Stdnt Opt", "gradingBasisLong": "Letter or S/U grades", "simpleCombinations": [{"subject": "COGST", "catalogNbr": "2621", "type": "C"}], "sessionCode": "6WK", "sessionBeginDt": "06/23/2025", "sessionEndDt": "08/05/2025", "sessionLong": "Six Week Summer", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}], "description": "Throughout history, metaphors drawn from technology of the time have been proposed to understand how the mind works. While Locke likened the newborn's mind to a blank slate, Freud compared the mind to hydraulic and electro-magnetic systems. More recently, many have endorsed Turing's proposal that the mind is a computer. Why is this idea attractive and what exactly is a computer? Is it at all plausible that the cells of your brain are computing? Could a computer ever really have a mind, beliefs, emotions and conscious experiences? What are these mysterious things anyway? Could a machine ever count as a person and make choices based on its own free will? Is it really so clear that we have this kind of free will?", "catalogBreadth": null, "catalogDistr": "(ETM-AS) (KCM-AG)", "catalogEnrollmentPriority": null, "catalogLang": "", "catalogForbiddenOverlaps": null, "catalogAttribute": "", "catalogWhenOffered": "Fall.", "catalogComments": "", "catalogPrereqCoreq": "", "catalogPrereq": null, "catalogCoreq": null, "catalogFee": null, "catalogSatisfiesReq": "", "catalogPermission": "", "catalogCourseSubfield": "", "catalogOutcomes": null, "acadCareer": "UG", "acadGroup": "AS", "lastTermsOffered": "2025FA, 2025SU, 2024FA, 2023SP", "exploreCriteriaIds": []}]}, "message": null, "meta": {"copyright": "Cornell University, Office of the University Registrar", "rosterDttm": "2025-10-15T19:07:41-0400", "catalogDttm": "2025-07-09T19:07:04-0400"}}